8,8,8,8,8,4,4,4,8,8,2,2,2,2,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,4,4,8,8,2,2,2,2,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,4,4,8,8,6,6,8,8,8,8,2,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,6,6,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,6,6,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,6,6,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,6,6,6,6,6,6,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The previous program got every hub side and every chosen colour right. All of its errors come from the orientation step: some shapes docked turned the wrong way.\n\n**Why the orientation was wrong.** Take the 3‑shape in pair 1, `.33./3333/3..3`. Its right column (`.`,`3`,`3`) is also two adjacent filled cells. So the unrotated shape already passed my test \"the hub‑facing edge has exactly two adjacent cells\", and it was docked without turning. The 1‑shape in pair 2 and the 7‑shape in pair 3 failed the same way.\n\n**What the expected outputs show.** In each case the edge that touches the bar is the shape's *narrow end*. Its two cells sit exactly in the middle of that edge. For example, pair 1's 3‑shape ends up in rows 8–11, and its touching cells are rows 9–10, the centre of that span. So the correct test is: the facing edge has exactly two adjacent cells, **and they are centred** (they start at position (edge length − 2)/2).\n\n**Checking this against all pairs:**\n- Pair 1: the 3‑shape's top row `.33.` is the only centred edge, so it gets rotated so that row faces the left bar.\n- Pair 3: the 2×4 block of colour 1 can only stand vertically. That gives an edge of length 2 starting at 0, which counts as centred.\n- Pairs 2 and 3: the 1‑shape and the 7‑shape are likewise only accepted with their `.XX.` row facing the bar.\n\n**Checking it on the test shapes.** Each matching shape has exactly one centred two‑cell edge:\n- 1‑shape: its right column `.11.`. It is rotated so that column faces down onto the top bar.\n- 4‑shape: its bottom row `.44.` faces the left bar.\n- 2‑shape: its top row `.22.` faces the right bar.\n- 6‑shape: its left column `..66..` faces the bottom bar.\n- The 3‑shape has no matching hub side and is removed, as before.\n\n**The fix.** Only the edge condition in the rotation loop changes. The rest of the construction stays the same: find the hub, erase the loose shapes, then dock each matching shape one at a time.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    hub = None\n    for y in range(1, H - 2):\n        for x in range(1, W - 2):\n            if (g[y:y+2, x:x+2] != bg).any():\n                continue\n            t = g[y-1, x:x+2]; b = g[y+2, x:x+2]\n            l = g[y:y+2, x-1]; r = g[y:y+2, x+2]\n            ok = True\n            for s in (t, b, l, r):\n                if s[0] == bg or s[0] != s[1]:\n                    ok = False\n            if ok:\n                hub = (y, x, int(t[0]), int(b[0]), int(l[0]), int(r[0]))\n                break\n        if hub:\n            break\n    out = g.copy()\n    if hub is None:\n        emit(out, \"No hub found; grid unchanged.\")\n        return out\n    y, x, ct, cb, cl, cr = hub\n    hubcells = {(y-1, x), (y-1, x+1), (y+2, x), (y+2, x+1),\n                (y, x-1), (y+1, x-1), (y, x+2), (y+1, x+2)}\n    mask = g != bg\n    for c in hubcells:\n        mask[c] = False\n    objs = []\n    for col in np.unique(g[mask]):\n        for cells in components(mask & (g == col), diagonal=True):\n            objs.append((int(col), cells))\n    for col, cells in objs:\n        for (cy, cx) in cells:\n            out[cy, cx] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Erase every loose shape; only the hub with its four coloured sides remains.\")\n    sides = {ct: 'top', cb: 'bottom', cl: 'left', cr: 'right'}\n    for col, cells in objs:\n        if col not in sides:\n            continue\n        side = sides[col]\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        m = np.zeros((y1-y0+1, x1-x0+1), bool)\n        for (cy, cx) in cells:\n            m[cy-y0, cx-x0] = True\n        placed = False\n        for k in range(4):\n            T = np.rot90(m, k)\n            h, w = T.shape\n            if side == 'top':\n                edge = T[h-1, :]\n            elif side == 'bottom':\n                edge = T[0, :]\n            elif side == 'left':\n                edge = T[:, w-1]\n            else:\n                edge = T[:, 0]\n            idx = np.nonzero(edge)[0]\n            if len(idx) != 2 or idx[1] != idx[0] + 1:\n                continue\n            if 2 * idx[0] + 2 != len(edge):\n                continue  # the two-cell end must be centred (narrow tip)\n            i0 = idx[0]\n            if side == 'top':\n                oy, ox = y - 1 - h, x - i0\n            elif side == 'bottom':\n                oy, ox = y + 3, x - i0\n            elif side == 'left':\n                oy, ox = y - i0, x - 1 - w\n            else:\n                oy, ox = y - i0, x + 3\n            for a in range(h):\n                for b2 in range(w):\n                    if T[a, b2] and 0 <= oy+a < H and 0 <= ox+b2 < W:\n                        out[oy+a, ox+b2] = col\n            placed = True\n            break\n        if placed:\n            emit(out, f\"Rotate the colour-{col} shape so its centred two-cell tip faces the hub and attach it to the {side} side.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: each matching shape docked on its hub side, unmatched shapes removed.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 1: the 3‑shape's top row `.33.` is the only centred edge, so it gets rotated so that row faces the left bar."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is rotated so that column faces down onto the top bar."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**The fix.** Only the edge condition in the rotation loop changes."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"All of its errors come from the orientation step: some shapes docked turned the wrong way."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"tated shape already passed my test \"the hub‑facing edge has exactly two adjacent cells\", and it was docked without turning."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The rest of the construction stays the same: find the hub, erase the loose shapes, then dock each matching shape one at a time."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Checking it on the test shapes.** Each matching shape has exactly one centred two‑cell edge:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The rest of the construction stays the same: find the hub, erase the loose shapes, then dock each matching shape one at a time."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Final grid: each matching shape docked on its hub side, unmatched shapes removed."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"That gives an edge of length 2 starting at 0, which counts as centred."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"c8cf2dc19f6ea01919e2c1ebb42ecf3a8d4f14fa241f2ca5cba40ae03f79f2a3","task_sha256":"533c8ca834f5b216c9ef0151e16d61d5a75b0d2b0430d532a7524c3d11219ada","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
